**From “5 minutes ma aauxu” to waiting for hours**

> **“5 minutes ma aauxu.”**

I used to believe that meant five minutes.

Maybe seven, if there was traffic.

Now, when I hear it, I don't even know what unit of time I am supposed to understand.

Ten minutes? Thirty minutes? An hour?

I have heard “5 minutes ma aauxu” enough times that five minutes has slowly stopped feeling like a measurement of time and started feeling more like a promise that may or may not have any connection with the clock.

But before I complain about everyone else, I should probably admit something.

I am not always on time either.

I have always been somewhat of a late person. I am not the guy who arrives everywhere 30 minutes early with a stopwatch in his hand. So this isn't a story about me being perfectly punctual while everyone around me is late.

What changed was something else.

I used to believe that when someone gave you a time, that time meant something.

If you said 9:00, I understood 9:00.

Over time, after repeatedly waiting for people, I started adjusting my expectations.

And eventually, I started adjusting my own behavior too.

## I Used to Trust the Time People Gave Me

There was a time when I thought punctuality was simply a matter of keeping your word.

If someone told me to be somewhere at 9:00 AM, I would try to get there around that time. Sometimes I would even arrive early.

If I knew I was going to be late, even by a few minutes, I could get anxious.

Not because I was obsessed with the clock.

I just didn't want someone else to be standing somewhere waiting for me.

But then I started noticing how often the opposite happened.

I would arrive.

And wait.

And wait.

Someone would say:

> “9 baje hai hai, time mai aau hai.”

So I would arrive around 8:50 or 8:55.

Then 9:00 would come.

Nothing.

9:05.

Still nothing.

9:15.

So I would call.

And eventually:

> “Ah, bato ma aaudai chu.”

*I'm on the way.*

Sometimes they actually were on the way.

And sometimes I had a feeling that “on the way” meant they had finally started getting ready.

That was when I started asking myself:

**What does “on time” actually mean to us?**

## The Five-Minute Problem

There is something almost magical about the sentence:

> **“5 minutes ma aauxu.”**

Five minutes can become ten.

Ten can become twenty.

Twenty can somehow become forty.

And by the time the person actually arrives, you have already gone through several emotional stages.

First, you wait patiently.

Then you check your phone.

Then you check the road.

Then you call them.

Then you get:

> “Aairako xu, 5 minutes.”

At this point, you don't even know whether to laugh or get angry.

The funny thing is that I have also started changing because of this.

I don't always believe the time anymore.

If someone tells me 9:00, a small part of my brain now calculates the realistic arrival time.

Not because I want to be late.

Because I have learned to expect waiting.

And that's where I think something interesting happened to me.

Nobody directly told me to become less strict about time. Repeated experiences taught me to expect less from the clock.

## When 9:00 Stopped Meaning 9:00

This might be the biggest change in my own thinking.

I used to measure time according to the clock.

Now, sometimes, I measure it according to expectations.

If someone tells me 9:00, I don't necessarily imagine them standing there at 9:00 anymore.

I imagine traffic.

Getting ready.

Finding the keys.

Finishing another conversation.

Calling someone else.

And eventually:

> “Ah, bato ma aaudai chu.”

The clock hasn't changed.

My expectation has.

And that is probably the part that bothers me the most.

Because I don't want to become someone who automatically assumes that everyone will be late.

But repeated experiences make it difficult not to.

## The Family Picnic Where I Thought Everyone Was Waiting for Us

There was one family picnic that made me realize how complicated this whole thing could be.

It was the day of the picnic, and everyone was excited because it had been a while since the family had gone somewhere together.

The bus was reserved for around 7:00 AM. The plan was to gather everyone at different pickup points and then head toward the destination.

And there we were, still doing things at home as 7:00 AM was approaching.

I started getting anxious.

> “Let's go, we're already late.”

In my head, I imagined everyone had already reached their pickup points.

I imagined them standing there waiting for us.

I imagined someone looking at the time and wondering why we hadn't arrived yet.

So I kept telling everyone that we needed to leave.

Eventually, we left.

And then came the funny part.

When we reached the pickup point, we found out that the people whose houses were actually near the stop hadn't even arrived yet.

There I was, worrying that we were making everyone wait, while some of the people we were supposed to pick up were still at home.

One of the brothers jokingly said something like:

> “Next time fine halnu parchha.”

*Next time, there should be a fine.*

Everyone laughed.

I laughed too.

But I was also thinking:

> Wait... I was panicking about being late, and we're actually early?

That moment was funny, but it also made me realize something.

Sometimes my expectations about time were becoming stricter than reality itself.

I wasn't necessarily becoming more punctual.

I was becoming more anxious about other people's punctuality.

And sometimes, I was completely wrong about who was actually late.

## The Government Office Taught Me Something Different About Time

Another experience that changed how I think about time happened when I went with a friend to a government office for the driving licence trial.

Our online appointment said that our turn would start from **11:00 AM**. We actually went there much earlier, around **7:00 AM**, because we had to practice. We practiced, had some food, and then came back around **10:10–10:25 AM**, expecting that our scheduled time would mean something.

It didn't.

People who had appointments earlier than us were still waiting. There were already a lot of people around, and there wasn't a clear system where you could confidently look at the time and know when your turn would come.

At one point, people started shouting at the guard. But honestly, it felt like a waste of energy. The guard was just doing his job, and even he knew that there were still many people waiting. He told us that there were people who had already been waiting for more than an hour, while others were still being called for practice.

That's when I heard someone say something that stayed with me:

> **“I am not scared of failing and paying the fee, but I am scared of wasting my time when there is no proper time system.”**

That sentence made sense to me.

The frustrating part wasn't only whether someone would pass or fail. It was the uncertainty.

You can prepare for an exam.

You could accept paying the fee and trying again.

You can prepare for a journey.

But standing in a queue for hours without knowing when your turn would actually come is a different kind of frustration.

And I remember thinking about the **11:00 AM** appointment.

What exactly was 11:00 AM supposed to mean?

Was it the time we should arrive?

The time the process should start?

Or just a rough idea that our turn would happen sometime around then?

That experience made me realize that a time written on a screen doesn't automatically make a system punctual.

And maybe that's part of why I started thinking about time differently.

I began to understand that sometimes the problem isn't that people don't have a time.

It's that **the time doesn't actually tell you what will happen.**

## Then There Was Chitlang

Another experience made me look at time from a completely different angle.

A group of us were planning a trip to Chitlang. One of the people involved had come from France to Nepal for a vacation.

As the trip got closer, our group chat suddenly became much more active.

People were being added.

Some people were leaving.

Messages were appearing everywhere.

Plans were changing.

Eventually, the trip was organized and we reached the resort.

The interesting part was the schedule.

The barbecue was planned for around 8 PM.

Dinner was at 9 PM.

At first, I thought:

> “Okay... that's very specific.”

The person who had come from France seemed much more conscious about the schedule. They wanted things to happen according to the planned time.

8 PM meant barbecue.

9 PM meant dinner.

And so on.

But for the rest of us, this was supposed to be a break.

We weren't going to Chitlang for a business meeting.

We weren't attending a conference.

We weren't trying to catch a flight.

We were going there to relax.

So suddenly, the exact schedule started feeling strange.

One side was thinking:

> “We planned this time, so let's follow it.”

The other side was thinking:

> “We came here to relax. Why are we looking at the clock every few minutes?”

That experience made me realize something.

Maybe the problem isn't always that one side values time and the other doesn't.

Sometimes people simply have different expectations of what a particular occasion is supposed to be.

A work meeting and a holiday don't necessarily need to feel the same.

But at the same time, if a group agrees on a particular time, shouldn't that agreement mean something?

I still don't have a perfect answer to that.

## The Office Experiment: How Can I Be Late and Still Be Early?

My office experience made this whole thing even more confusing.

I've had different phases.

Sometimes I arrived at a reasonable time. Not extremely early, not extremely late. Just... normal.

Then things changed.

New people joined.

The environment changed.

The routine changed.

And slowly, my own attitude toward arrival time started changing too.

At one point, I became curious.

What would happen if I didn't arrive on time?

Not because I wanted to cause a problem.

I was simply curious.

I wanted to see what would happen if I arrived later than I normally did.

So I started coming late.

The first time, I came around 20 minutes late.

And I was still early.

I thought:

*Okay... maybe that was just luck.*

Another time, I came around 30 minutes late.

Still early.

At this point, I was genuinely confused.

Then I tried around 40 minutes.

And somehow...

I was still early.

I remember thinking:

**How am I late and early at the same time?**

It sounds ridiculous when I write it down.

But that was probably one of the moments that changed how I thought about workplace timing.

The time I had considered “late” was apparently still earlier than when many people normally arrived.

And suddenly, my own definition of being late didn't seem very useful anymore.

I had spent so much time worrying about whether I was arriving late that I hadn't stopped to ask a more important question:

**Late compared to what?**

The clock?

The official working time?

The usual arrival time?

Or the time everyone in the office had silently accepted as the “actual” starting time?

I still don't come late every day.

It isn't like I suddenly decided punctuality doesn't matter.

But that experience made me realize how easily our standards change depending on the people around us.

If everyone arrives early, arriving on time feels normal.

If everyone arrives late, arriving on time can start feeling unusually early.

And eventually, the definition of “late” becomes less about the clock and more about the people around you.

## “Nepali Time Ta Ho Ni”

There is a phrase that makes this whole thing easier to dismiss:

> **“Nepali Time ta ho ni.”**

Sometimes it's said as a joke.

Sometimes as an excuse.

Sometimes almost proudly.

Someone arrives late and people laugh:

> “Tapai ta time mai aaipugnu bhayecha!”

As if arriving on time is the unusual thing.

And maybe that's where the problem becomes bigger than one person being late.

When lateness becomes expected, people start planning around lateness.

If an event says 7:00, people may mentally assume it will actually begin later.

If someone says they are coming in five minutes, nobody necessarily expects five minutes.

And once everyone expects this, the cycle becomes self-sustaining.

The person who arrives on time waits.

The person who arrives late sees that people are still waiting.

The next time, they may not feel the need to hurry as much.

And the person who waited eventually starts arriving late too.

The expectation creates the behavior, and the behavior strengthens the expectation.

At least, that's how it sometimes feels to me.

## But Is It Really About Not Valuing Time?

I don't think every late person is disrespectful.

That would be too easy.

Kathmandu traffic exists.

Public transportation is unpredictable.

Road conditions change.

Unexpected things happen.

Sometimes you genuinely cannot control when you will arrive.

I've been late too.

The difference, at least in my mind, is between being late because something happened and treating the agreed time as something that never really mattered in the first place.

If something unexpected happens, tell me.

If you're going to be late, tell me.

If you haven't even left yet, maybe don't tell me you're “five minutes away.”

Because then it isn't really about the five minutes anymore.

It's about the expectation I built around your words.

## When Time Suddenly Matters

Something else I have noticed is how quickly time becomes important when **we** need it.

If someone has another appointment afterward, suddenly the schedule matters.

If there is a bus to catch, the departure time matters.

If there is an exam, the reporting time matters.

If there is a flight, nobody says:

> “Nepali Time ta ho ni, let's reach the airport an hour late.”

The urgency appears when there is a consequence.

That makes me wonder:

**Do we value time itself, or do we value time when there is a penalty for wasting it?**

Maybe that's part of the reason punctuality can feel different in different situations.

When being late costs money, an opportunity, an exam, or a flight, people suddenly become very aware of the clock.

But when the cost is another person's waiting time, perhaps the consequence feels less visible.

And that's the part I find unfair.

Because someone's waiting time is still time.

## The Person Who Waits Is Paying Too

This is probably what changed my thinking the most.

When someone is late, we usually think about their delay.

They were stuck in traffic.

They were getting ready.

They had something else to finish.

But there is another person in the story.

The person waiting.

If you are 30 minutes late, you haven't only lost 30 minutes.

Someone else may have lost 30 minutes too.

Maybe they could have studied.

Worked.

Rested.

Eaten.

Gone somewhere else.

Done something they actually wanted to do.

The clock doesn't stop for the person waiting.

It just keeps moving.

And nobody gives that time back.

## So, Have I Become Part of “Nepali Time”?

This is the part I find slightly uncomfortable.

After complaining about people being late, I have also started adjusting myself.

I sometimes arrive later because I expect others to arrive later.

I sometimes give myself extra time because I know the announced time may not be the real time.

And then I wonder:

**Am I adapting to reality, or am I helping maintain it?**

Maybe both.

If everyone expects everyone else to be late, who is actually going to start being on time?

Maybe someone has to be the person who arrives at 9:00 and waits.

But after enough waiting, even that person gets tired.

And that is how the cycle continues.

## I Don't Think I Have the Answer

I started thinking about this because I was frustrated.

I wanted to know why someone could casually say “5 minutes” when I knew those five minutes could easily become half an hour.

But the more I thought about it, the less simple the answer became.

Time isn't only about clocks.

It is also about expectations.

It is about circumstances.

It is about priorities.

It is about how seriously we treat commitments to other people.

And maybe it is also about what we have become used to.

I don't expect everyone to live according to a stopwatch.

Life doesn't work that way.

Traffic happens.

Plans change.

Unexpected things happen.

But I do think there is a difference between being unable to arrive on time and simply not considering someone else's time important enough to plan around.

## Maybe Five Minutes Should Mean Five Minutes

I don't know if my perception of time will ever go back to what it was.

Maybe I will always leave a little later because I expect someone else to be late.

Maybe I'll always hear:

> “5 minutes ma aauxu.”

And quietly add another 20 minutes in my head.

But I still miss the simpler version of time.

When 9:00 meant 9:00.

When “I'll be there in five minutes” meant I could actually expect someone in five minutes.

When arriving early felt like being responsible rather than volunteering to wait.

Maybe the real problem isn't that we sometimes arrive late.

The real problem is when we stop being surprised by it.

Because once lateness becomes normal, punctuality starts looking unusual.

And perhaps that's the change I have experienced myself.

**I didn't stop believing in the value of time.**

**I just started believing that everyone doesn't value it in the same way.**

And honestly, I'm still trying to decide whether that is something I should accept or something I should never have gotten used to in the first place.
